WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.648 --> 00:00:04.928
- What's up Believe Nation.

00:00:04.928 --> 00:00:06.370
I started the Mentor
Me Series with the goal

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:08.675
to hang around people who've
done a lot more than us,

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.868
who've been a lot more
successful, and by spending

00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:13.713
a little more time with
them, hopefully some of their

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:15.363
mindset, their views, their beliefs,

00:00:15.363 --> 00:00:18.207
seep into us to help us
become the best version

00:00:18.207 --> 00:00:20.270
of ourselves, so today,
we're going to learn

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:22.579
from Sundar Pichai and
his road to becoming

00:00:22.579 --> 00:00:24.266
the CEO of Google.

00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:25.316
Mentor Me Sundar.

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:27.583
And as always guys, when
you're watching the videos,

00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:29.802
if you see something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:29.802 --> 00:00:31.328
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:31.328 --> 00:00:34.251
and put quotes around it, so
other people can be inspired,

00:00:34.251 --> 00:00:36.192
and if you write it down,
it's much more likely to stick

00:00:36.192 --> 00:00:38.025
with yourself as well.

00:00:38.914 --> 00:00:42.081
(inspirational music)

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.767
- The thing which attracted
me to Google and to Internet

00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:04.267
in general is that it's a great equalizer,

00:01:05.745 --> 00:01:10.122
and so to me, I've always
been struck by the fact

00:01:10.122 --> 00:01:13.674
that Google Search worked the same.

00:01:13.674 --> 00:01:16.150
As long as you had access to
computer with connectivity,

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.991
if you were to relocate
anywhere or if you were a

00:01:18.991 --> 00:01:21.422
president of Stanford or Harvard.

00:01:21.422 --> 00:01:24.062
And to me, you know, I
want Google to strive

00:01:24.062 --> 00:01:26.578
to push to do that, not
just build technology

00:01:26.578 --> 00:01:28.411
for a certain segment.

00:01:30.328 --> 00:01:34.044
For me, it matters that, you
know, we drive technology

00:01:34.044 --> 00:01:37.144
as an equalizing force, as an enabler,

00:01:37.144 --> 00:01:39.561
to everyone around the world.

00:01:45.151 --> 00:01:48.115
- What motivated you to switch
fields from first metallurgy

00:01:48.115 --> 00:01:50.054
to management to computer science,

00:01:50.054 --> 00:01:53.402
and did you ever feel incompetent
or even a bit insecure

00:01:53.402 --> 00:01:57.222
by working with people who
were even more qualified

00:01:57.222 --> 00:01:59.555
in that field than you were.

00:02:01.055 --> 00:02:03.940
- You know, my background,
I was always interested

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.329
in technology, and the
first chance I could get

00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:08.884
the program, I learned
programming, so for me,

00:02:08.884 --> 00:02:10.492
I never thought of it as switching fields

00:02:10.492 --> 00:02:13.662
as much as when the
Internet started happening,

00:02:13.662 --> 00:02:17.207
it took me awhile to
realize the power of it,

00:02:17.207 --> 00:02:20.924
and you know it became obvious
to me for the first time

00:02:20.924 --> 00:02:23.686
we are going to have
something which could connect,

00:02:23.686 --> 00:02:26.860
one day, all of humanity
and profoundly transform

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.199
what people did, so I
wanted to be a part of that,

00:02:30.199 --> 00:02:32.792
be a part of that journey, so
that's how I thought of it.

00:02:32.792 --> 00:02:35.933
So I didn't think of it
necessarily as switching careers.

00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:37.786
I would actually encourage all of you

00:02:37.786 --> 00:02:40.424
if at some point in your life,
you have to work with people

00:02:40.424 --> 00:02:42.818
where you feel a bit insecure.

00:02:42.818 --> 00:02:44.757
That's essential because
that means you're working

00:02:44.757 --> 00:02:46.657
with people who are better than you,

00:02:46.657 --> 00:02:49.388
and who are pushing you, right?

00:02:49.388 --> 00:02:52.507
So always encourage, if
you actually very secure

00:02:52.507 --> 00:02:54.866
in what you do, that means
you're doing something

00:02:54.866 --> 00:02:57.729
comfortable, and you're
not pushing yourself.

00:02:57.729 --> 00:03:00.414
There were many many times
I felt working with people

00:03:00.414 --> 00:03:03.878
in a group, am I doing
enough, these people seem

00:03:03.878 --> 00:03:06.056
much better than me, and
I think that's an inherent

00:03:06.056 --> 00:03:07.473
part of learning.

00:03:12.723 --> 00:03:17.311
You know I interviewed at
Google on April first, 2004,

00:03:17.311 --> 00:03:18.671
which is April Fool's Day,

00:03:18.671 --> 00:03:21.351
(audience laughs)

00:03:21.351 --> 00:03:24.734
and Google had just announced Gmail,

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:27.742
and it was invite only, and
so, but people weren't exactly

00:03:27.742 --> 00:03:31.412
sure whether it was an April Fool's joke.

00:03:31.412 --> 00:03:33.933
So I remember doing my
interviews during the day

00:03:33.933 --> 00:03:35.873
and people kept asking me
what do you think of Gmail,

00:03:35.873 --> 00:03:38.275
but I hadn't had a chance to use it.

00:03:38.275 --> 00:03:41.612
I thought it was an April Fool's joke.

00:03:41.612 --> 00:03:44.330
So my first three interviews
I couldn't answer it well

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:46.144
because I hadn't used the product.

00:03:46.144 --> 00:03:48.288
It was only in the fourth
interview when someone asked me,

00:03:48.288 --> 00:03:49.644
have you seen Gmail?

00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:53.155
I said no, and so he
actually showed it to me.

00:03:53.155 --> 00:03:55.335
And then the fifth interviewer asked,

00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:57.523
what do you think of Gmail,
and I was able to start

00:03:57.523 --> 00:03:59.751
answering it then, so
the final four interviews

00:03:59.751 --> 00:04:02.474
I actually told them about
what I think of Gmail

00:04:02.474 --> 00:04:04.612
and how to improve Gmail,

00:04:04.612 --> 00:04:07.202
and that's my interview experience.

00:04:07.202 --> 00:04:09.429
Somebody also took me to
have ice cream in the middle,

00:04:09.429 --> 00:04:10.837
which showed me Google is

00:04:10.837 --> 00:04:12.653
a very very different place as well.

00:04:12.653 --> 00:04:14.549
So that was how my interview process went.

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:16.162
- Is it true that everyone at that time

00:04:16.162 --> 00:04:18.062
was (mumbles).

00:04:18.062 --> 00:04:20.284
- I was one of the first
people, I think, you know,

00:04:20.284 --> 00:04:22.598
I joined Google when we
were over a thousand people,

00:04:22.598 --> 00:04:24.744
and Larry just at that
point, it was the first time

00:04:24.744 --> 00:04:27.298
he had stopped interviewing
people, so I still joke

00:04:27.298 --> 00:04:29.435
around that I got into
Google because Larry

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.102
didn't interview me.

00:04:36.936 --> 00:04:40.735
I had a dream, just like
I'm sure many many Indians,

00:04:40.735 --> 00:04:43.902
dreamt of being a Cricketer.
- You did?

00:04:45.147 --> 00:04:46.465
(audience laughs)
- Yeah.

00:04:46.465 --> 00:04:48.404
I used to be a huge fan of, sorry I'm old,

00:04:48.404 --> 00:04:50.916
but I used to be a huge fan of Gavaskar,

00:04:50.916 --> 00:04:52.852
you know, when he was playing.

00:04:52.852 --> 00:04:55.435
(crowd cheers)

00:04:56.892 --> 00:04:58.738
And later on Sachin when he played,

00:04:58.738 --> 00:05:01.707
but so I always had a dream.

00:05:01.707 --> 00:05:05.327
On a more serious note,
I always loved technology

00:05:05.327 --> 00:05:10.270
growing up, and so I had
dreams of just not exactly

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:14.718
what I would do, but I
wanted to do, at the time,

00:05:14.718 --> 00:05:17.032
I used to read about what was
happening in Silicon Valley,

00:05:17.032 --> 00:05:19.752
and I wanted to go be a part of it.

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:21.525
So I didn't exactly dream of carrier

00:05:21.525 --> 00:05:23.540
as much as a destination.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.401
- You're sitting in (mumbles) in a room

00:05:25.401 --> 00:05:29.070
that wasn't quite as
fancy as this hall is,

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.682
and there is no Google, so how are you

00:05:30.682 --> 00:05:32.948
keeping in touch with what
Silicon Valley is doing

00:05:32.948 --> 00:05:35.507
and how are you dreaming?

00:05:35.507 --> 00:05:38.021
- Wow, I have to think
about that for a minute.

00:05:38.021 --> 00:05:41.651
There was no Internet too,
and it was pre all that.

00:05:41.651 --> 00:05:43.651
You know mostly reading.

00:05:44.912 --> 00:05:49.079
My uncle had gone to the
states, and I was interested

00:05:52.103 --> 00:05:55.196
in semiconductors and so
on, and William Shockley,

00:05:55.196 --> 00:05:58.495
the person who kind of
invented semiconnectors

00:05:58.495 --> 00:06:01.254
was from Silicon Valley,
so I had read a story

00:06:01.254 --> 00:06:05.460
about him staying, working
through, at Stanford,

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:08.639
close to Christmas Eve,
and so stuff like that.

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:11.077
For what I was interested in,

00:06:11.077 --> 00:06:14.160
I knew that's where it was happening.

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:23.823
- But what is Sundar
Pichai like, as a leader?

00:06:23.823 --> 00:06:25.144
- You know, I think--

00:06:25.144 --> 00:06:26.710
- What is your leadership style.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:29.550
- When you turn it on
something the scale of Google,

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.300
we have now over 60,000
people, and you know,

00:06:34.593 --> 00:06:37.251
you rely on other strong leaders.

00:06:37.251 --> 00:06:39.812
A lot of what I do, is you
know, I have an outstanding

00:06:39.812 --> 00:06:41.786
leadership team.

00:06:41.786 --> 00:06:45.365
It's learning to let go and
really empowering people

00:06:45.365 --> 00:06:48.404
at all levels of the organization.

00:06:48.404 --> 00:06:50.840
And trusting them to do the right thing.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.117
As a leader, a lot of your job

00:06:53.117 --> 00:06:54.986
is to make those people successful.

00:06:54.986 --> 00:06:57.097
It's less about trying to be successful

00:06:57.097 --> 00:07:00.929
and more about making
sure you have good people,

00:07:00.929 --> 00:07:04.616
and your work is to remove
barriers, remove roadblocks

00:07:04.616 --> 00:07:07.918
for them so that they can be
successful in what they do.

00:07:07.918 --> 00:07:10.511
And so that's how I've
always thought about it.

00:07:10.511 --> 00:07:14.053
And also valued teamwork quite a bit.

00:07:14.053 --> 00:07:16.855
I think it's really important
to build organizations

00:07:16.855 --> 00:07:19.984
where people actually
want to work together.

00:07:19.984 --> 00:07:21.961
Everything comes out of that.

00:07:21.961 --> 00:07:25.250
So setting up collaborative
cultures is another big thing

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:26.651
I try to focus on.

00:07:26.651 --> 00:07:28.214
- So it's not about one
person, it's about the team.

00:07:28.214 --> 00:07:29.411
- That's right.

00:07:29.411 --> 00:07:30.607
- And if you team succeeds,
you also succeed with them.

00:07:30.607 --> 00:07:31.690
- Absolutely.

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:40.241
You know, I would, I
really encourage people

00:07:40.241 --> 00:07:43.205
to, you know, I think,
there's a lot of pressure

00:07:43.205 --> 00:07:45.183
in today's system.

00:07:45.183 --> 00:07:47.832
I get surprised people
start preparing for IT

00:07:47.832 --> 00:07:51.935
in their eighth grades, and
that's a bit shocking to me.

00:07:51.935 --> 00:07:56.102
I hope, I hope, you know, as
people are approaching things

00:07:58.449 --> 00:08:01.088
they are really taking the time,

00:08:01.088 --> 00:08:02.783
doing things in a deeper way.

00:08:02.783 --> 00:08:04.632
Understanding things deeper.

00:08:04.632 --> 00:08:06.858
Learning by doing things, and you know,

00:08:06.858 --> 00:08:09.576
I think it's important to
remember it's a long road.

00:08:09.576 --> 00:08:11.844
Setbacks actually don't matter.

00:08:11.844 --> 00:08:14.810
I think a lot of times when I was younger,

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.443
people would say this person
didn't get into this college

00:08:17.443 --> 00:08:19.382
or something, and that's
the end of the road.

00:08:19.382 --> 00:08:21.811
Life is so different from that.

00:08:21.811 --> 00:08:25.312
And so I think it's
important to keep your hopes,

00:08:25.312 --> 00:08:28.357
keep your dreams, and try to follow them.

00:08:28.357 --> 00:08:32.725
I think most of how life
plays out is up to you,

00:08:32.725 --> 00:08:36.149
not up to what happens outside of you,

00:08:36.149 --> 00:08:37.922
and I think it's important
to keep that in mind

00:08:37.922 --> 00:08:39.733
and take the long-term view.

00:08:39.733 --> 00:08:41.139
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:41.139 --> 00:08:42.497
I'd love to know what did you think?

00:08:42.497 --> 00:08:43.852
What did you learn from Sunday today

00:08:43.852 --> 00:08:46.001
that is going to have the
biggest impact on your life

00:08:46.001 --> 00:08:47.158
or your business?

00:08:47.158 --> 00:08:48.923
What was your favorite clip and why?

00:08:48.923 --> 00:08:50.445
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:08:50.445 --> 00:08:52.218
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:08:52.218 --> 00:08:53.445
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Julio Velez.

00:08:53.445 --> 00:08:56.706
Julio thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:08:56.706 --> 00:08:59.095
Your One Word, and for making
that book summary video

00:08:59.095 --> 00:09:00.830
on your YouTube video channel.

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:03.420
I really really really
appreciate your support.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.841
- So this book is called Your
One Word by Evan Carmichael.

00:09:06.841 --> 00:09:08.366
- So thank you guys again for watching.

00:09:08.366 --> 00:09:09.602
I believe in you.

00:09:09.602 --> 00:09:11.042
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:09:11.042 --> 00:09:13.271
and whatever your one word is.

00:09:13.271 --> 00:09:15.604
Much love.
I'll see you soon.

